      <h2 class="section-title">3.4 Data Gathering and Requirement</h2>
      <p class="justified-text">
        To ensure that PinjamGeng authentically addresses the real challenges experienced across Universiti Poly-Tech Malaysia, a rigorous requirement gathering exercise was conducted. A structured questionnaire was distributed via Google Forms to campus stakeholders including student club executives (MCSC, MPP, Sports Club), event organizers, and faculty laboratory technicians. The quantitative and qualitative findings directly validated the project problem statements and informed the system's core capabilities.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-title">3.4.1 User Requirement Analysis (Survey Results)</h3>
      <p class="justified-text">
        The survey gathered responses across twelve essential dimensions regarding equipment borrowing practices, pain points, and expectations:
      </p>

      <!-- Survey Questions 1 & 2 -->
      <div style="border: 1px solid #000; padding: 12px; margin: 15px 0;">
        <p><strong>Figure 3.2: User Questionnaire 1 – Stakeholder Role Distribution</strong></p>
        <p class="justified-text"><em>Question: What is your role in campus events?</em></p>
        <p class="justified-text"><strong>Result:</strong> 72% Student Club Committee / Event Organizers, 16% Faculty Lecturers / Club Advisors, 12% Equipment Administrators / Lab Technicians. This distribution confirms that student event organizers represent the predominant user base requiring an intuitive borrowing portal, while administrative technicians require robust control mechanisms.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="border: 1px solid #000; padding: 12px; margin: 15px 0;">
        <p><strong>Figure 3.3: User Questionnaire 2 – Frequency of Equipment Loan</strong></p>
        <p class="justified-text"><em>Question: How often does your club or department borrow equipment?</em></p>
        <p class="justified-text"><strong>Result:</strong> 44% Monthly, 32% Several times per semester, 16% Weekly, 8% Annually. The high frequency of loan activity emphasizes that equipment borrowing is a routine campus necessity requiring an automated, permanent platform.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="border: 1px solid #000; padding: 12px; margin: 15px 0;">
        <p><strong>Figure 3.4: User Questionnaire 3 – Encountered Difficulties with Manual Forms</strong></p>
        <p class="justified-text"><em>Question: What difficulties do you experience when using manual paper loan forms? (Multi-select)</em></p>
        <p class="justified-text"><strong>Result:</strong> 84% Slow approval process and repeated counter visits, 76% Inability to check whether gear is available in advance, 64% Lost physical application slips, 48% Forgetting necessary cables and accessories. This strongly validates Problem Statement 1.2.1 and 1.2.2.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="border: 1px solid #000; padding: 12px; margin: 15px 0;">
        <p><strong>Figure 3.5: User Questionnaire 4 – Double-Booking &amp; Availability Conflicts</strong></p>
        <p class="justified-text"><em>Question: Have you ever experienced equipment being unavailable or double-booked on your event day?</em></p>
        <p class="justified-text"><strong>Result:</strong> 68% Yes, frequently during event weeks; 24% Yes, occasionally; 8% Never. This overwhelming 92% incidence rate confirms that lack of real-time inventory visibility is a critical operational failure in the existing manual process.</p>
      </div>

