      <h2 class="section-title">3.6 System and Application Modelling</h2>
      <p class="justified-text">
        System modeling provides the formal abstract blueprint representing system workflows, behavioral dynamics, and database architectures. Modeling ensures that software components operate cohesively, eliminating architectural flaws prior to and during system construction.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-title">3.6.1 Flowchart</h3>
      <p class="justified-text">
        Two distinct, publication-standard black-and-white flowcharts were developed to specify the execution paths for both user classifications: the Administrator and the Student Borrower.
      </p>
      <p class="justified-text">
        Figure 3.110 details the Administrator Flowchart. The workflow initiates with credential authentication. Upon successful login, the administrator selects from four primary operational branches: (1) Booking Management (reviewing requests, approving or rejecting applications, allocating physical stock, and issuing loan slips); (2) Inventory &amp; Package Administration (adding, updating, and removing equipment and bundle packages); (3) Return Desk &amp; Inspection (inspecting returned assets, auditing physical condition, logging automatic maintenance tickets for damaged items, or restocked healthy gear); and (4) Reports &amp; Analytics (generating date-filtered utilization summaries and exporting PDF/CSV reports).
      </p>

      <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="footer-left">CC101</span>
        <span class="footer-right">Page 91 of 154</span>
      </div>
    </div>

